1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,720
This is a ZX Spectrum loading a program.

2
00:00:08,260 --> 00:00:12,840
And this is the same program, on the same
computer, from the same kind of cassette.

3
00:00:16,630 --> 00:00:18,450
It finishes in half the time.

4
00:00:18,850 --> 00:00:20,730
Nothing in the hardware changed.

5
00:00:21,070 --> 00:00:23,350
No faster tape, no faster recorder.

6
00:00:24,153 --> 00:00:27,713
The only thing that changed
was the way the computer listened.

7
00:00:28,452 --> 00:00:31,852
So how did 8-bit computers
learn to load games faster?

8
00:00:32,752 --> 00:00:35,972
And if it was that easy,
why didn't they do it from the start?

9
00:00:37,742 --> 00:00:40,762
In an earlier video,
we saw how the Spectrum stores bits

10
00:00:40,992 --> 00:00:42,542
as pulses on the tape.

11
00:00:43,150 --> 00:00:45,570
A short pair of pulses means zero.

12
00:00:46,110 --> 00:00:48,250
A pair twice as long means one.

13
00:00:48,516 --> 00:00:51,596
That gives about fourteen hundred bits
every second.

14
00:00:52,016 --> 00:00:56,576
A game that fills the 48K memory
takes around five minutes.

15
00:00:57,897 --> 00:01:00,337
Now make every pulse half as long.

16
00:01:00,979 --> 00:01:03,479
The computer still tells short from long.

17
00:01:04,179 --> 00:01:07,499
But twice as many bits
pass the tape head every second.

18
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:10,680
That's the whole trick
behind a turbo loader.

19
00:01:11,340 --> 00:01:13,360
And it isn't just a thought experiment.

20
00:01:14,189 --> 00:01:19,869
In 1984, the game Skool Daze loaded its
data at twice the Spectrum's normal speed.

21
00:01:22,699 --> 00:01:25,799
So why didn't the Spectrum
load that fast by itself?

22
00:01:26,571 --> 00:01:31,271
The built-in loader is stored in the ROM,
the memory chip the computer starts from.

23
00:01:31,811 --> 00:01:34,071
It doesn't measure a pulse in seconds.

24
00:01:34,882 --> 00:01:39,062
It counts. After each edge of the signal,
it waits a moment,

25
00:01:39,162 --> 00:01:41,442
then counts how long until the next one.

26
00:01:41,956 --> 00:01:46,316
One single number in the ROM
decides where zero ends and one begins.

27
00:01:46,765 --> 00:01:48,765
And that number leaves a lot of room.

28
00:01:49,525 --> 00:01:53,945
A zero can stretch by more than forty
per cent and still be read as a zero.

29
00:01:54,265 --> 00:01:58,305
That margin is what lets a worn tape
on a cheap recorder still load.

30
00:01:59,569 --> 00:02:01,549
And the computer wasn't the limit.

31
00:02:02,249 --> 00:02:06,369
The Spectrum checks the tape signal
about sixty thousand times a second.

32
00:02:07,027 --> 00:02:08,547
The cassette was the limit.

33
00:02:09,327 --> 00:02:11,387
Shorter pulses mean higher tones.

34
00:02:12,187 --> 00:02:15,787
At double speed, a zero
moves from two kilohertz to four.

35
00:02:15,921 --> 00:02:17,481
And high tones are exactly what

36
00:02:17,711 --> 00:02:20,321
a cheap recorder,
or a slightly misaligned head,

37
00:02:20,551 --> 00:02:21,841
loses first.

38
00:02:22,524 --> 00:02:25,184
A Spectrum magazine in 1986 reckoned

39
00:02:25,414 --> 00:02:28,684
most people could manage
at least twice the normal speed.

40
00:02:29,244 --> 00:02:31,864
How much further depended on the recorder.

41
00:02:34,514 --> 00:02:36,734
But turbo had a stranger problem.

42
00:02:37,247 --> 00:02:40,027
If the computer only knows
the slow format,

43
00:02:40,207 --> 00:02:43,307
how does it load the program
that teaches it the fast one?

44
00:02:44,450 --> 00:02:46,570
The answer is at the start of the tape.

45
00:02:47,290 --> 00:02:50,810
The first part is recorded
in the ordinary, slow format.

46
00:02:51,503 --> 00:02:54,683
It's short, and the ROM loads
it like any other program.

47
00:02:55,175 --> 00:02:57,455
Then that little program takes over.

48
00:02:58,195 --> 00:03:00,355
In the simplest case,
it's a copy of the ROM's

49
00:03:00,585 --> 00:03:03,455
own loading routine,
with a few numbers changed.

50
00:03:04,254 --> 00:03:07,274
In Skool Daze,
the changes sit in three places.

51
00:03:08,094 --> 00:03:11,994
A shorter wait, a new starting count,
a new dividing line.

52
00:03:12,480 --> 00:03:16,360
So the ROM loads the program
that does the ROM's job, only faster.

53
00:03:17,099 --> 00:03:19,999
It's a bit like downloading
a faster downloader.

54
00:03:20,419 --> 00:03:22,339
The slow step gets you the tool.

55
00:03:22,679 --> 00:03:24,179
The tool does the rest.

56
00:03:26,739 --> 00:03:29,179
Commercial games used the same idea.

57
00:03:29,979 --> 00:03:34,939
One well-known example was Speedlock,
first used by Ocean in 1984.

58
00:03:35,583 --> 00:03:38,143
One of its authors said he wrote it
because he was fed up

59
00:03:38,373 --> 00:03:40,443
with how long the standard loader took.

60
00:03:41,162 --> 00:03:42,722
But here's the surprise.

61
00:03:43,442 --> 00:03:46,622
Speedlock was only about one and a half
times as fast.

62
00:03:47,561 --> 00:03:49,621
The first day
of Daley Thompson's Decathlon

63
00:03:49,851 --> 00:03:53,721
took three minutes twenty seconds,
where the same data at normal speed

64
00:03:53,951 --> 00:03:55,421
would take four minutes forty-seven.

65
00:03:56,703 --> 00:04:00,023
Later versions were only about
a fifth faster than the ROM.

66
00:04:00,103 --> 00:04:03,003
Because speed wasn't the only job.

67
00:04:04,091 --> 00:04:07,811
From 1983, copying programs
could read a game's header

68
00:04:08,041 --> 00:04:10,751
and copy the whole game onto another tape.

69
00:04:11,377 --> 00:04:14,797
A custom format
gave those programs nothing to read.

70
00:04:15,441 --> 00:04:19,241
Early Speedlock even chopped
its lead-in tone into short bursts,

71
00:04:19,581 --> 00:04:22,581
so the ROM's own loader
wouldn't recognise it at all.

72
00:04:22,931 --> 00:04:24,331
And you could hear it.

73
00:04:24,751 --> 00:04:28,671
A Speedlock tape loaded with clicks,
and jerking stripes around the screen.

74
00:04:31,941 --> 00:04:35,221
It didn't stop anyone copying
a tape from one deck to another.

75
00:04:35,301 --> 00:04:39,581
But it did give publishers
a reason to write their own loaders.

76
00:04:42,181 --> 00:04:46,041
On the Commodore 64,
the reason to switch was much bigger.

77
00:04:46,715 --> 00:04:51,115
Its built-in routine recorded
everything twice, as a safety copy.

78
00:04:51,855 --> 00:04:55,035
A forty-kilobyte game took
about twelve and a half minutes.

79
00:04:55,551 --> 00:04:58,291
And here, the recorder
did almost nothing clever.

80
00:04:58,991 --> 00:05:01,891
It just turned the sound
into clean on and off pulses.

81
00:05:02,753 --> 00:05:06,013
The computer timed every pulse itself,
in software.

82
00:05:06,775 --> 00:05:09,515
So a faster loader
was just a different program.

83
00:05:10,684 --> 00:05:12,644
In 1985, Compute!

84
00:05:12,874 --> 00:05:14,684
magazine tested one called TurboTape.

85
00:05:15,943 --> 00:05:18,283
Once it had found the program on the tape,

86
00:05:18,443 --> 00:05:21,323
it loaded twelve kilobytes
in twenty-eight seconds.

87
00:05:22,055 --> 00:05:25,875
Commodore's own floppy disk drive
needed thirty-four seconds.

88
00:05:26,567 --> 00:05:28,347
A cassette, beating a floppy disk.

89
00:05:30,387 --> 00:05:33,627
And because the computer was doing
all the listening itself,

90
00:05:33,947 --> 00:05:35,927
it could do other things in between.

91
00:05:36,648 --> 00:05:39,848
Loaders such as Freeload
gave reading the tape priority.

92
00:05:40,268 --> 00:05:46,068
Whatever processor time was left went to
music, smooth scrolling, even small games.

93
00:05:46,773 --> 00:05:48,643
With Mastertronic's Invade-a-Load,

94
00:05:48,733 --> 00:05:52,313
you played a Space Invaders clone
while the real game loaded.

95
00:05:54,833 --> 00:05:57,953
The Atari 8-bit computers
were a different story.

96
00:05:58,683 --> 00:06:00,763
Here, the recorder did the listening.

97
00:06:01,903 --> 00:06:04,403
Inside it were two filters,
one for each tone,

98
00:06:04,483 --> 00:06:07,943
and a circuit that handed the computer
finished ones and zeros.

99
00:06:08,637 --> 00:06:11,057
The standard was six
hundred bits a second,

100
00:06:11,137 --> 00:06:14,357
and the filters limited
how much faster it could go.

101
00:06:14,963 --> 00:06:19,643
Clever software could nudge that up
a little, at best to about twelve hundred.

102
00:06:19,723 --> 00:06:23,143
But to go much faster,
you had to open the recorder.

103
00:06:23,714 --> 00:06:26,334
And in Czechoslovakia, people did.

104
00:06:26,973 --> 00:06:31,433
A floppy drive there cost two to three
times as much as the computer itself.

105
00:06:32,233 --> 00:06:34,833
So most Atari owners
there stayed with tape.

106
00:06:35,477 --> 00:06:41,617
In 1987, a Prague student from the local
Atari club designed Turbo 2000.

107
00:06:42,399 --> 00:06:44,639
It added one new path for the signal.

108
00:06:45,519 --> 00:06:47,579
A wire inside the recorder took the sound

109
00:06:47,809 --> 00:06:50,779
straight from its amplifier,
before the filters,

110
00:06:50,879 --> 00:06:54,179
through one small chip,
and onto the line to the computer.

111
00:06:54,977 --> 00:06:59,397
A switch, flipped by the computer,
chose between the old path and the new.

112
00:07:00,023 --> 00:07:02,503
The computer now timed the pulses itself.

113
00:07:02,583 --> 00:07:05,803
Around two thousand three hundred bits
a second.

114
00:07:06,123 --> 00:07:08,303
Almost four times as fast.

115
00:07:09,683 --> 00:07:12,203
Turbo wasn't one universal format.

116
00:07:12,915 --> 00:07:14,955
Sometimes you changed the program.

117
00:07:15,615 --> 00:07:17,795
Sometimes, you had to change the machine.

118
00:07:20,805 --> 00:07:22,565
And faster wasn't free.

119
00:07:23,221 --> 00:07:23,501
Compute!

120
00:07:23,731 --> 00:07:27,061
warned that a TurboTape recording
would sometimes refuse to load

121
00:07:27,291 --> 00:07:31,101
on a different recorder, with its head
aligned slightly differently.

122
00:07:31,829 --> 00:07:35,969
The standard save, it said,
was much slower, but extremely reliable.

123
00:07:36,661 --> 00:07:39,941
Recording everything twice
wasn't Commodore being lazy.

124
00:07:40,641 --> 00:07:42,521
It was the price of loading reliably.

125
00:07:45,101 --> 00:07:48,221
But a different loader
could also do something unexpected.

126
00:07:49,081 --> 00:07:51,641
It could read a tape
that the built-in one refused.

127
00:07:52,473 --> 00:07:56,353
On the Sharp MZ-800,
a computer that found its way

128
00:07:56,583 --> 00:08:02,093
to Czechoslovakia in the late 1980s,
owners knew a program called Turbo Copy.

129
00:08:02,767 --> 00:08:05,607
A game would fail to load,
with a checksum error.

130
00:08:06,187 --> 00:08:09,307
Owners found Turbo Copy
could often load it anyway.

131
00:08:09,769 --> 00:08:12,769
Then you saved it at normal speed
onto a fresh cassette,

132
00:08:13,189 --> 00:08:15,529
and the computer loaded it normally again.

133
00:08:16,009 --> 00:08:18,709
It felt like repairing a broken game.

134
00:08:19,760 --> 00:08:22,780
So how could one loader read
what the other couldn't?

135
00:08:23,912 --> 00:08:26,552
The Sharp's ROM recorded
every block twice,

136
00:08:26,832 --> 00:08:28,992
at about twelve hundred bits a second.

137
00:08:29,383 --> 00:08:31,083
And it never measured a pulse.

138
00:08:31,883 --> 00:08:35,623
After each pulse began,
it waited a fixed moment, and looked once.

139
00:08:36,350 --> 00:08:37,830
Still high?

140
00:08:37,953 --> 00:08:39,153
That's a one.

141
00:08:39,522 --> 00:08:40,722
Already low?

142
00:08:40,869 --> 00:08:42,129
That's a zero.

143
00:08:42,839 --> 00:08:44,899
A stopwatch, set in advance.

144
00:08:46,073 --> 00:08:50,753
On an old tape, or a different recorder,
the pulses came back a little rounder.

145
00:08:51,113 --> 00:08:53,933
Then that single look
could see the wrong level.

146
00:08:54,513 --> 00:08:56,693
One wrong bit spoils the checksum.

147
00:08:56,773 --> 00:08:59,553
If both copies fail, the load fails.

148
00:09:00,528 --> 00:09:02,608
Turbo Copy listened differently.

149
00:09:03,228 --> 00:09:06,268
It timed every pulse
with the computer's timer chip.

150
00:09:06,641 --> 00:09:10,001
And before each block,
it listened to the lead-in tone,

151
00:09:10,181 --> 00:09:13,841
and set its dividing line
from the pulses it was actually hearing.

152
00:09:14,481 --> 00:09:17,321
Its stopwatch was set
to the tape in front of it.

153
00:09:18,505 --> 00:09:20,945
And the repair wasn't a copy of the sound.

154
00:09:21,545 --> 00:09:26,085
Turbo Copy kept the bytes, and wrote
a brand new, cleanly timed signal.

155
00:09:26,688 --> 00:09:28,348
The data hadn't been lost.

156
00:09:28,988 --> 00:09:31,688
The usual way of reading it
just wasn't good enough.

157
00:09:32,628 --> 00:09:34,688
Turbo Copy could also
save the game with turbo,

158
00:09:34,918 --> 00:09:39,808
at two or three times the normal speed,
with its own small loader in front.

159
00:09:42,678 --> 00:09:44,978
And that's what all of these
have in common.

160
00:09:45,654 --> 00:09:47,534
The cassette stayed the same.

161
00:09:47,914 --> 00:09:49,914
What changed was the way it was read.

162
00:09:51,864 --> 00:09:54,444
Today, we expect games to load instantly.

163
00:09:55,304 --> 00:09:57,924
In the 1980s,
loading was part of the game.

164
00:09:58,284 --> 00:10:02,504
You listened to the tape.
You watched the stripes. You waited.

165
00:10:03,316 --> 00:10:06,576
And sometimes, a different loader
made the same cassette behave

166
00:10:06,806 --> 00:10:08,856
like a completely different machine.

167
00:10:09,913 --> 00:10:12,273
The computer didn't need a faster tape.

168
00:10:12,773 --> 00:10:14,693
It needed a better way to listen.

169
00:10:17,273 --> 00:10:19,113
So here's a question for you.

170
00:10:19,653 --> 00:10:21,833
Did you use turbo loaders back then?

171
00:10:22,093 --> 00:10:23,273
How do you remember them?

172
00:10:23,353 --> 00:10:26,693
Did they fail more often?
Let me know in the comments.

173
00:10:27,445 --> 00:10:30,205
And finally, the whole turbo tape,
start to finish.

174
00:10:31,225 --> 00:10:33,505
First the part at normal speed,
then the turbo.

175
00:10:34,105 --> 00:10:37,085
For a program this small,
turbo doesn't pay off.

176
00:10:37,625 --> 00:10:40,065
The loader takes longer
than the time it saves.

177
00:10:40,845 --> 00:10:42,615
For a whole game, it could save minutes.
